#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# exp_to_trig

#% text
# `exp_to_trig(e)` writes exponentials of imaginary arguments by Euler's formula, `exp(im*x) = cos(x) + im*sin(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exponentials of real arguments are left as they are.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(im*x))
#% output
#= cos(x) + im*sin(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(-im*x))
#% output
#= cos(x) - im*sin(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig((exp(im*x) + exp(-im*x))/2)
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp(x) + exp(-x))
#% output
#= exp(x) + exp(-x)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A difference of logarithms that is an inverse function (the midpoint of the two arguments a positive number):

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(1 + im*x) - log(1 - im*x))
#% output
#= 2im*atan(x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(2 + x) - log(2 - x))
#% output
#= 2*atanh((1/2)*x)

#% cell
exp_to_trig(log(z + sqrt(z^2 + 1)))
#% output
#= asinh(z)

#% text
# A complex exponent with a real part:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(exp((1 + im)*t))
#% output
#= cos(t)*exp(t) + im*exp(t)*sin(t)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `trig_to_exp` goes the other way:

#% cell
exp_to_trig(trig_to_exp(cos(x)))
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `trig_to_exp`, `exp`, `cos`, `sin`
